# IS A LANDING GATE RED ON MAIN? -- THE GATE's three workflows, exactly what the pre-push red gate reads
# (scripts/hooks/pre-push `GatingWorkflows`, `agentctl ci-why`, .github/gating-checks.tsv): Main, Identity model
# convergence, and Identity takes (the takes rows, split from convergence into a gating workflow of their own on
# 2026-11-08). The scrape recording gates nothing since 2026-10-27 (97bb6d835): neither its capture (Record scrape
# fixtures) nor its hermetic replay (Verify scrape recording), whose reds are information (`agentctl ci-why`). Until
# 2026-11-15 this rule still watched Verify scrape recording and not Identity takes: 6 of its 8 episodes in the week to
# 2026-11-15 were the recording's verify, which holds no push, and a red Identity takes, which does, said nothing.
# While one is red, every production push needs a `Fixes-Red:` trailer or waits; a red nobody is fixing blocks
# everyone, and nothing said so but the next refused push. (CI build-time regressions are not this file's.)
# The gate's finer reading -- a run red only in an aggregate (`verdict complete`) or in an `unproven` job holds no push --
# is not in the run's series, which carries only its conclusion: such a run fires here too, and ci-why says why it does
# not gate.
#
# THE SERIES: `kinowo_ci_run_seconds{workflow,ref,measure,conclusion}`, which every workflow's last job (`timings`,
# `if: !cancelled()`, scripts/ci/ci_timings.py) pushes over OTLP once per run, stamped at the RUN'S START, with the
# run's conclusion (its jobs' worst while it is still running). One sample per run, hours apart, so an instant
# selector sees nothing: `timestamp()` over a 5-minute-step subquery reads each run's start back, and the newest
# failed run on main is compared with the newest successful one. A cancelled run decides nothing, as in the gate.
groups:
  - name: ci-gates
    rules:
      # THE NEWEST DECIDED RUN OF A GATE ON MAIN FAILED, AND NO LATER ONE PASSED, FOR TWO HOURS. A run still going
      # pushes nothing until it ends, so a fix in flight keeps this firing until it lands green -- what the gate does.
      # The 3-day window bounds the subquery; a gate red for longer than that is long past an alert.
      - alert: LandingGateRed
        expr: |
          max by (workflow) (max_over_time(timestamp(kinowo_ci_run_seconds{ref="main",measure="wall",conclusion="failure",workflow=~"Main|Identity model convergence|Identity takes"})[3d:5m]))
            >
          (
            max by (workflow) (max_over_time(timestamp(kinowo_ci_run_seconds{ref="main",measure="wall",conclusion="success",workflow=~"Main|Identity model convergence|Identity takes"})[3d:5m]))
            or
            0 * max by (workflow) (max_over_time(timestamp(kinowo_ci_run_seconds{ref="main",measure="wall",conclusion="failure",workflow=~"Main|Identity model convergence|Identity takes"})[3d:5m]))
          )
        for: 2h
        labels:
          severity: warning
          domain: ci
        annotations:
          summary: "{{ $labels.workflow }} has been red on main for 2 hours"
          description: >-
            The newest finished {{ $labels.workflow }} run on main failed and no later one has passed, for two
            hours. Every production push now needs a `Fixes-Red:` trailer naming that run, or waits for green.
            `~/bin/agentctl ci-why` names the run, its failed jobs and whether the failure is a test or the CI
            itself.
